    <h2>Three systems</h2>
    <p>
      Govern.ad states that three education systems operate in the Principality:
      the Andorran system, the Spanish system, and the French system. Each
      follows its own programmes. The official education-structure pages set out
      how maternal, primary, secondary and baccalaureate stages sit beside one
      another.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the Spanish and French systems the Government requires
      <strong>Formació Andorrana</strong>: Catalan, and Andorra’s history,
      geography, culture and institutions, adapted to each system from early
      years through later stages. That is the official integration course, not a
      private extra.
    </p>

    <h2>University</h2>
    <p>
      The
      <a href="https://www.uda.ad/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >Universitat d’Andorra</a
      >
      is the public university. Its site places the campus in Sant Julià de
      Lòria and describes programmes aligned with the European Higher Education
      Area. We list it in the
      <a href="/directory/universitat-d-andorra">directory</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>International teaching</h2>
    <p>
      Alongside the three public systems, at least one British-curriculum
      international school publishes its own programmes:
      <a href="/directory/british-college-andorra"
        >The British College of Andorra</a
      >. That is a sourced listing, not a ranking of every private option.
    </p>

    <p>
      Language in daily life is wider than the classroom. Catalan is the
      official language; Spanish and French are widely used. Immigration files
      can ask for Catalan at renewal — confirm the level then required on
      <a href="https://www.govern.ad/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >Govern.ad</a
      >, not from a remembered brochure.
    </p>
    <p>
      See
      <a href="/living-in-andorra">living in Andorra</a>
      and
      <a href="/official-sources">official sources</a>.
    </p>
